unstruct
	Prepares output channel
ref	channel
	for replay of data (a replay job).
	<p>
	Normally the
ref	background
	parameter would be set to <code>kSMNullChannelId</code>. If
	however, this parameter has assigned to it the channel id of
	another output channel, then the signal generated on the output
	channel
ref	channel
	will be combined with the data currently
	being output on
ref	background.
	Note that a channel must not be reconfigured while it is in
	use as a background channel for other channels.
	<p>
	To keep a continuous background signal while playing a
	mixture of different recorded signals and silence, start the
	replay only once and use
fnref	sm_put_replay_data
	as necessary to feed it data for each signal. To produce
	silence, simply stop sending data for a suitable period (the
	channel will report an underrun when it starts sending
	silence).
	<p>
	The
ref	volume
	parameter determines the gain applied to the replayed data and
	has a range of at least -24 to +8 in dB.
	<p>
	The
ref	agc
	parameter controls whether automatic gain control is applied to
	the replayed data. If
ref	agc
	is non-zero then automatic gain control is applied. Even if this
	is the case, the output level is still governed by volume.
	<p>
	The
ref	speed
	parameter determines rate of replay and is a percentage value
	expressing the rate of replay compared with the normal replay
	rate. Only certain speeds are supported, so the speed specified
	is rounded to the nearest supported value. The following speeds
	are currently supported:
	<p><table border cellpadding=5>
	<tr> <th>Ratio</th> <th>Percentage</th> </tr>
	<tr> <td>2:1</td> <td>200</td> </tr>
	<tr> <td>3:2</td> <td>150</td> </tr>
	<tr> <td>4:3</td> <td>133</td> </tr>
	<tr> <td>1:1</td> <td>100</td> </tr>
	<tr> <td>3:4</td> <td>75</td> </tr>
	<tr> <td>2:3</td> <td>67</td> </tr>
	<tr> <td>1:2</td> <td>50</td> </tr>
	</table>
	<p>
	An application should not rely on a speed being rounded to a
	specific value. Other speeds may also be supported, so a
	requested speed may round to a value nearer than expected.
	<p>
	If
ref	speed
	is set to zero, the data is replayed at an unadjusted rate.
	<p>
	The
ref	type
	parameter determines the format of data that will played back
	over the timeslot.
	<p>
	<a name="compatcode">Note that, for compatibility</a> with
	earlier releases of Prosody, many other values are permitted for
	the
ref	type
	field. These compatibility values specify a combination of
	data type and sampling rate. When one of these is used in
	the
ref	type
	field, the
ref	sampling_rate
	field must be zero, and the actual rate used will be as
	listed here. They are:
