	Condition input signal with, for example, an echo cancellation algorithm
apifn	sm_condition_input
params	sm_condition_input_parms condp
	The channel on whose input conditioning is to be performed.
field	tSMChannelId channel in
	The reference channel, if any, which is to be used for
	conditioning.
field	tSMChannelId reference in
	What sort of reference to use.
enumstart	kSMInputCondRef
	No reference signal.
enum	kSMInputCondRefNone
	Use reference input signal.
enum	kSMInputCondRefUseInput
	Use reference output signal.
enum	kSMInputCondRefUseOutput
enumend	reference_type in
	The type of conditioning to perform.
enumstart	kSMInputCond
	Disable input conditioning. This also disables any
	redirection of a signal such as may have been set up using
ref	alt_data_dest.
enum	kSMInputCondNone
	Remove echo from the input with respect to the specified reference
	signal.
	Requires the modules
modref	echocan
	and
modref	passthru
	to have been downloaded.
enum	kSMInputCondEchoCancelation
enumend	conditioning_type in
	Unused.
field	tSM_INT conditioning_param in
	A channel to receive the resulting conditioned signal, if any
	form of conditioning is enabled.
field	tSMChannelId alt_data_dest in
	What kind of alternative destination to use.
enumstart	kSMInputCondAltDest
	Conditioned signal not redirected - conditioned signal replaces
	signal input to
ref	channel.
enum	kSMInputCondAltDestNone
	Conditioned signal replaces signal input to
ref	alt_data_dest.
enum	kSMInputCondAltDestInput
	Conditioned signal replaces signal being output on
ref	alt_data_dest.
enum	kSMInputCondAltDestOutput
enumend	alt_dest_type in
	For Aculab use only: selects an echo cancellation test mode.
	If non-zero, creates simulated echo.
	This is not part of the official API and may be changed
	arbitrarily.
field	int ectest_type in
	For Aculab use only: the amount of signal to mix in as
	simulated echo. Only used when
ref	ectest_type
	is 1.
field	float ectest_gain in
	For Aculab use only: the delay (in samples) for the
	simulated echo. Only used when
ref	ectest_type
	is 1.
field	int ectest_delay in
	For Aculab use only: the length of the echo generation
	filter. Only used when
ref	ectest_type
	is 2.
field	unsigned ectest_flen in
	For Aculab use only: the echo generation filter. Only used when
ref	ectest_type
	is 2.
field	float *ectest_filt in ectest_filt
unstruct
	Applies or disables conditioning to the signal input to channel
ref	channel
	with respect to reference signal on channel
ref	reference.
	The input signal to be conditioned is called the
	<em>primary</em>.
	The reference may either be the input to a channel or the output
	from a channel. In particular, it can be the output from
ref	channel
	(but not its input). Note that Prosody switching functions (such
	as
fnref	sm_switch_channel_input
	or
fnref	sm_channel_datafeed_connect)
	must not be used on a reference while it is in use.
	<p>
	If input signal conditioning is enabled, the conditioned version
	of the input is generated and is directed to one of several places.
	<ul>
	<li>If
ref	alt_dest_type
	is
ref	kSMInputCondAltDestNone
	then when other speech
	processing functions such as conferencing or recording are
	performed on
ref	channel,
	the conditioned version of the input signal will be used.
	<li>If
ref	alt_dest_type
	is
ref	kSMInputCondAltDestInput
	then when such speech processing functions are performed on
ref	alt_data_dest,
	the conditioned version of the input signal
	will be used. (If
fnref	sm_condition_input
	is used on such a channel, the result is undefined).
	<li>If
ref	alt_dest_type
	is
ref	kSMInputCondAltDestOutput
	then the conditioned signal appears as the output from the channel
ref	alt_data_dest,
	which may be the same channel as
ref	channel.
	</ul>
	<p>
	Note that Prosody switching functions (such as
fnref	sm_switch_channel_output
	or
fnref	sm_channel_datafeed_connect)
	must not be used on the destination while echo cancellation is
	being performed.
	<p>
	All channels specified by
ref	channel,
ref	alt_data_dest,
	and
ref	reference
	will need to be processed by the same module. This can be
	ensured through the use of
fnref	sm_channel_alloc_placed
	<p>
	The two commonest configurations are:
	<dl>
	<dt>Cancelling the echo from a prompt being played
	<dd>
	In this situation you use one channel, playing the prompt on it,
	using its output as the reference (so
ref	reference
	=
ref	channel
	and
ref	reference_type
	=
ref	kSMInputCondRefUseOutput),
	and recording its input.
	<dt>Stand alone echo cancellation
	<dd>
	Here you want to send the conditioned signal to an external
	destination, rather than recording it. You need at least two
	channels because you need two inputs - one for the
	<em>primary</em> and one for the <em>reference</em> - and one
	output, while a single Prosody channel can have at most one
	input and one output. One way to arrange this is to have
ref	channel,
	whose input is the primary, be specified as the output (i.e.
ref	alt_data_dest
	=
ref	channel
	and
ref	alt_dest_type
	=
ref	kSMInputCondAltDestOutput),
	with a different channel being used for
ref	reference.
	</dl>
version	Ps
	Not supported by Prosody S.
version	+
explain
	0
retval
error	ERR_SM_DEVERR - device error
error	ERR_SM_BAD_PARAMETER - illegal parameter value

	Adjust input conditioning
apifn	sm_condition_adjust
params	sm_condition_adjust_parms condp
	The channel to which conditioning is being applied.
field	tSMChannelId channel in
	What sort adjustment to perform.
enumstart	kSMInputCondAdjust
	Select whether linear or non-linear echo cancellation with muting is
	performed. This adjustment is only valid if the input
	conditioning is currently
	<a href="apifn-sm_condition_input.html#field-kSMInputCondEchoCancelation">
	kSMInputCondEchoCancelation</a>.
	The
ref	adjust_value
	field selects which to use, with the value zero selecting linear
	echo cancellation, and other values selecting non-linear
	cancellation with muting. Non-linear echo cancellation with muting
	suppresses the signal when it calculates that, after having the echo
	removed, there is no significant signal remaining. This mode of
	operation is suitable for large-scale conferencing and some IVR
	applications, where it is important that the signal is completely
	muted whenever the caller is not speaking. Negative values represent
	the lower limit in dBm0. Positive values cause the default value
	of -42dBm0 to be used.
enum	kSMInputCondAdjustNonLinearWithMuting
	Select whether linear or non-linear echo cancellation with comfort 
	noise generation is performed. This adjustment is only valid if the
	input conditioning is currently
	<a href="apifn-sm_condition_input.html#field-kSMInputCondEchoCancelation">
	kSMInputCondEchoCancelation</a>.
	The
ref	adjust_value
	field selects which to use, with the value zero selecting linear
	echo cancellation, and other values selecting non-linear
	cancellation with comfort noise generation (CNG). In this mode
	the echo canceller replaces any residual traces of echo with
	"comfort noise". This is most appropriate to IP gateway applications
	where background noise is desirable to maintain the illusion of
	continuity. Negative values represent the threshold in dBm0.
	Positive values cause the default value of -42dBm0 to be used.
